Memory from the system: page mappings, and the C allocator for code that must interoperate
with it. The allocators of layer 4 (`ca::heap`, `page_allocator`, `libc_allocator`) are built on
these; application code normally uses those instead.

### Mappings

| Function | Does |
|---|---|
| `sys::page_size()` | the size of a page |
| `sys::mmap_anonymous(size, protection)` | fresh zeroed pages, private to the process |
| `sys::mmap_file(fd, offset, size, protection, sharing)` | a file's pages, `shared` or `copy_on_write` |
| `sys::mremap(mapping, size, remapping)` | grows or shrinks a mapping, `in_place` or `may_move` |
| `sys::mprotect(region, protection)` | changes the protection of pages |
| `sys::madvise(region, advice)` | `normal`, `random`, `sequential`, `will_need`, `dont_need` |
| `sys::msync(mapping)` | writes a shared file mapping back and waits |
| `sys::munmap(mapping)` | unmaps a mapping, or some pages of one |
| `sys::shm_create(size)`, `shm_attach(id, size)`, `shm_detach(mapping)`, `shm_remove(id)` | a System V shared memory segment, as X11's MIT-SHM shares with the X server |

A mapping is a `span<byte>`: functions return one and take one, so the size always travels with
the address. Regions must be page-aligned; an unaligned one fails with
`errc::invalid_argument`. `protection` holds the bits `read`, `write`, `execute` and `none`.
On Linux x86-64 the calls go straight to the kernel through `ca::syscall`, without libc.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"
#include "ca/span.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::usz page = ca::sys::page_size();
	auto block = ca::sys::mmap_anonymous(4U * page, ca::sys::protection::read | ca::sys::protection::write);
	if (!block)
		return 1;
	ca::span<ca::byte> pages = *block;
	if (pages[page] != 0U)
		return 2;
	pages[page] = 42U;

	auto grown = ca::sys::mremap(pages, 8U * page, ca::sys::remapping::may_move);
	if (!grown || grown->size() != 8U * page || (*grown)[page] != 42U)
		return 3;
	pages = *grown;

	if (!ca::sys::madvise(pages.suffix(4U * page), ca::sys::advice::dont_need))
		return 4;
	if (!ca::sys::mprotect(pages, ca::sys::protection::read))
		return 5;
	auto unaligned = ca::sys::mprotect(pages.sub(1U, page), ca::sys::protection::read);
	if (unaligned || unaligned.error() != ca::errc::invalid_argument)
		return 6;
	return ca::sys::munmap(pages) ? 0 : 7;
}
```

### The C allocator

`sys::mallocarray(count, size)` and `sys::reallocarray(block, count, size)` allocate with the
multiplication checked for overflow, aligned to `malloc_alignment`; `sys::free` releases
(nullptr does nothing). They exist for blocks that cross into C code, `realpath`'s result for
instance.

`move_paths.hpp` exposes the strategies ca's memcpy and memmove take, one function per size class
and instruction set (`ca::move_paths::bytes_0_to_15`, `forward`, `stream`, ...), each checking that
the size is in its range; benchmarks and tests call them to measure or verify one path alone.
The out-of-line ones (`forward` and after) need SSE2: an integer-only build has the inline size
classes only.
